  <h2 class="sources-heading">Sources</h2>
  <p class="text-[.82rem] text-muted leading-[1.7] mb-3">Breaking performance down by hold type, wall angle, and movement style rather than grade alone follows two converging lines of evidence: finger/grip strength is the strongest measurable performance determinant in the climbing-science literature, and unaddressed technique gaps -- not just physical capacity -- are a well-documented cause of plateaus.</p>
  <ol class="m-0 pl-[1.2rem] text-[.84rem] leading-[1.6] text-foreground [&>li+li]:mt-[10px]">
    <li>MacLeod, D. (2010). <em class="text-muted italic">9 Out of 10 Climbers Make the Same Mistakes: Navigation Through the Maze of Advice for the Self-coached Climber</em>. The book's central thesis -- that most climbers plateau from unaddressed technical/tactical errors, not lack of physical training -- is the reason this view segments by terrain and movement, not just raw send counts.</li>
    <li>Buraas, B. F., Brobakken, M. F., &amp; Wang, E. (2025). "Climbing performance in males: the importance of climbing-specific finger strength." <em class="text-muted italic">European Journal of Applied Physiology</em>, 125(10), 2823–2830. DOI: <a class="text-accent" href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s00421-025-05802-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">10.1007/s00421-025-05802-5 ↗</a></li>
    <li>Faggian, S., et al. (2024). "Sport climbing performance determinants and functional testing methods: A systematic review." <em class="text-muted italic">Journal of Sport and Health Science</em>, 14. <a class="text-accent" href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11904605/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov ↗</a></li>
    <li>Vigouroux, L., Domalain, M., &amp; Berton, E. (2011). "Effect of Object Width on Muscle and Joint Forces During Thumb–Index Finger Grasping." <em class="text-muted italic">Journal of Applied Biomechanics</em>, 27(3), 173–180. Biomechanical basis for why grip/hold width changes joint loading -- the reason hold type is tracked as its own dimension, not folded into a generic difficulty score.</li>
  </ol>

